// docs/sales/manual/content.en.js
//
// The words of "FieldQuo Sales Portal — Training Manual", in English.
//
// ══ Who this is written for ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// A trainer sitting beside a rep on their first day. Second person, short
// paragraphs, one screen at a time: what to press, what appears, why it is
// built that way. The reference guide (docs/sales/guide) says what the
// PRODUCT does; this says what the REP does, in the room they do it in.
//
// ══ The one rule for whoever edits this ═══════════════════════════════════
//
// Never type an on-screen label. Write {{app.salesQueue.claimBatch|count=25}}
// and the builder prints the catalogue's string for the language being
// built. A label typed by hand drifts from the screen the day someone
// renames the button, and a new rep reading a manual that disagrees with
// the screen stops trusting the manual. A key that does not exist fails the
// build — that is the point.
//
// Facts in here were read from the code on the day this was written
// (lib/sales/**, app/sales/**) and are cited in the builder's report. If the
// portal disagrees with a sentence here, the portal is newer: say so in
// #sales.
